Regex literals are not enabled — because the package is built in Swift 5 language
 mode (swift-tools-version:5.9), regex literals require either an opt-in via `-enable
-bare-slash-regex` or Swift 6 mode. Therefore, regex literals cannot be used. Instead
, use an NSRegularExpression or a manual scanning approach.

The simplest and most robust approach that doesn't use regex: manually scan the characters
. A tag is `#` followed by one or more alphanumeric/underscore/hyphen characters.
 This lets us avoid the regex availability issue and works on older toolchains as
 well.

Implementation:

```swift
public func extractTags(from text: String) -> [String] {
    var tags: [String] = []
    var current: [Character] = []
    var inTag = false
    func flush() {
        if inTag && !current.isEmpty {
            tags.append(String(current))
        }
        current.removeAll(keepingCapacity: true)
        inTag = false
    }
    for ch in text {
        if inTag {
            if ch.isLetter || ch.isNumber || ch == "_" || ch == "-" {
                current.append(ch)
            } else {
                flush()
            }
        } else if ch == "#" {
            inTag = true
        }
    }
    flush()
    return tags
}
```

Edge cases: a `#` not followed by a valid character (e.g., "#") — since current is
 empty, flush does nothing. Good.

One subtle behavior: "a#b" — in the old behavior, splitting on " " gives "a#b", which
 doesn't havePrefix "#", so no tag is produced. New behavior: "b" is produced. This
 is an intended extension (punctuation-attached tags). No problem.

Also, the old behavior on "#a b#c": the old one produced ["a", "c"] (because "#c".
hasPrefix → "c"). The new one does the same.

What about "#a."? Old: tag "a." — the old code included the trailing dot! New: "a
". This is a fix in the same spirit, and the user said the two examples must work
. Stripping punctuation is desirable. The existing tests only cover plain space-separated
 tags, so there's no regression.

